---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JwtStructureExplainer from '../../components/JwtStructureExplainer.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="JWT vs Session Authentication — Which to Use" description="A visual breakdown of the three parts of a JWT, plus how JWT-based auth compares to traditional session authentication.">
  <JwtStructureExplainer client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>JWT vs Session Authentication</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>JWT</th><th>Session</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Where state lives</td><td>Client (the token itself)</td><td>Server (session store / database)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scaling across servers</td><td>Easy — any server can verify the token</td><td>Needs a shared session store</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Revoking access instantly</td><td>Hard — token is valid until it expires</td><td>Easy — delete the session server-side</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payload visibility</td><td>Readable by anyone (signed, not encrypted)</td><td>Just an opaque ID — no visible data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Stateless APIs, microservices, mobile apps</td><td>Traditional web apps needing easy revocation</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The core tradeoff: JWTs scale easily but are hard to revoke early; sessions revoke instantly but require centralized state.</p>

  <h2>Why understanding the structure prevents security mistakes</h2>
  <p>A common and dangerous misconception is that JWTs are encrypted — they're signed, not encrypted, meaning anyone who intercepts a token can read its full payload. Understanding this distinction is essential before deciding what data is safe to include in a token.</p>

  <h2>How to use this explainer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Review each of the three JWT segments and what it contains.</li>
    <li>Keep in mind the payload is readable, not hidden, when designing what to put in it.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common JWT Structure Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Putting sensitive data directly in the payload</strong> (passwords, secrets, private personal data) — anyone with the token can decode and read it.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming a valid-looking JWT is automatically trustworthy</strong> without server-side signature verification — the format alone proves nothing about authenticity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This JWT Structure Explainer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Learning how JWTs work before implementing authentication</li>
    <li>Explaining to a team member why a JWT isn't a safe place for sensitive data</li>
    <li>Understanding what a decoded token's three segments actually represent</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>JWT vs Session FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is JWT better than session authentication?</strong> Not universally — JWTs scale better across distributed servers since no shared state is needed, but sessions make instant revocation trivial, which JWTs don't support without extra infrastructure like a blocklist.</p>
  <p><strong>Can you revoke a JWT before it expires?</strong> Not natively — the token remains valid until its expiry claim passes. Common workarounds include short expiry times with refresh tokens, or maintaining a server-side revocation list, which reintroduces some of the state JWTs are meant to avoid.</p>
  <p><strong>How is a JWT different from an encrypted token?</strong> A JWT's header and payload are just Base64URL-encoded (readable by anyone); only the signature is cryptographically protected, verifying the token wasn't tampered with — not hiding its contents.</p>
</ToolLayout>
